.TH PHYS 2 PDP11
.SH NAME
phys \- allow a process to access physical addresses
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B int phys(int segreg, int size, int physadr);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Note:
everything below describes PDP-11 segmentation hardware and none of the numbers
apply.  The gate exists and has a stub, and the page is kept as the record of what
the call was for.
.PP
.B The mapping unit here is a page of 1024 words,
not 8K bytes; a program sees 32 such pages through eight write-only page
registers, and the kernel keeps the only readable copy of the map.  See
doc/Memory_Mapping.md.
.PP
.B And there is a better way to reach physical memory on this system:
/dev/mem and /dev/kmem, which the kernel's memory driver backs, going through a
mapped window for any address a program could not otherwise name.
.PP
The argument
.I segreg
specifies a process virtual (data-space) address
range of 8K bytes starting
at virtual address
.IR segreg \(mu8K
bytes.
This address range is mapped into
physical address
.IR physadr \(mu64
bytes.
Only the first
.IR size \(mu64
bytes of this mapping is addressable.
If
.I size
is zero,
any previous mapping of this
virtual address range is nullified.
For example,
the call
.PP
	phys(6, 1, 0177775);
.PP
will map virtual addresses 0160000-0160077 into
physical addresses 017777500-017777577.
In particular,
virtual address 0160060 is the PDP-11 console
located at physical address 017777560.
.PP
This call may only be executed by the super-user.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
mem(4), doc/Memory_Mapping.md
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The function value zero is returned if
the physical mapping is in effect.
The value \-1 is returned
if not super-user,
if
.I segreg
is not in the range 0-7,
if
.I size
is not in the range 0-127,
or if
the specified
.I segreg
is already used for other than
a previous call to
.IR phys .
.SH BUGS
This system call is obviously very machine
dependent and very dangerous.
This system call is not considered
a permanent part of the system.
.SH ASSEMBLER
.B Note:
v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here \- a decimal call number and a
`\fBsys\fP \fIname\fP; \fIarg\fP ...' line.  Neither is how a call is made on this
machine.  The gate is one extracode \-
.B $77 SYS_phys
\- and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n\-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the
error number in r14.
.PP
.B The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.
The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is the only place a syscall number
appears on the user side.  See
.IR intro (2)
for the whole contract.
